      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of four-digit odd numbers (\\(A\\)) and the number of four-digit multiples of 5 (\\(B\\)), and then find the sum \\(A + B\\).\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(A\\), the number of four-digit odd numbers\n\nA four-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of the odd digits: 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (since the number cannot start with 0), and the second and third digits can be any digit from 0 to 9.\n\n- The first digit has 9 possible choices (1 through 9).\n- The second digit has 10 possible choices (0 through 9).\n- The third digit has 10 possible choices (0 through 9).\n- The fourth digit has 5 possible choices (1, 3, 5, 7, 9) to ensure the number is odd.\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit odd numbers is:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(B\\), the number of four-digit multiples of 5\n\nA number is a multiple of 5 if its last digit is either 0 or 5. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices), and the second and third digits can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices each).\n\n- The last digit has 2 possible choices (0 or 5).\n- The first digit has 9 possible choices (1 through 9).\n- The second digit has 10 possible choices (0 through 9).\n- The third digit has 10 possible choices (0 through 9).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 is:\n\\[\nB = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 2 = 1800\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(A + B\\)\n\nNow, we add the number of four-digit odd numbers and the number of four-digit multiples of 5:\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 1800 = 6300\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6300}\n\\]",
