      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the number of four-digit odd numbers (\\(A\\)) and the number of four-digit multiples of 5 (\\(B\\)), and then sum these two numbers.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(A\\)\nA four-digit odd number can be represented as \\( \\overline{abcd} \\), where \\(a, b, c, d\\) are its digits, and \\(d\\) is odd. The last digit \\(d\\) can be one of \\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\}, giving us 5 choices. The first digit \\(a\\) (it cannot be 0 for a four-digit number) has 9 choices (1 through 9). Each of the other two digits \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) has 10 choices (0 through 9).\n\nSo, the total number of four-digit odd numbers \\(A\\) is:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(B\\)\nA four-digit multiple of 5 must end in 0 or 5. We will calculate the number of four-digit multiples of 5 in each case and then sum them up.\n\n#### Case 1: Ending in 0\nA four-digit number ending in 0 can be represented as \\( \\overline{abcd} \\), where \\(d\\) is either 0 or one of \\{5, 7, 9\\}. So, \\(d\\) has 3 choices. The first digit \\(a\\) has 9 choices (1 through 9). Each of the other two digits \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) has 10 choices (0 through 9).\n\nSo, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 ending in 0 is:\n\\[\n9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 3 = 2700\n\\]\n\n#### Case 2: Ending in 5\nA four-digit number ending in 5 can be represented as \\( \\overline{abcd} \\), where \\(d\\) is either 5 or 7 or 9. So, \\(d\\) has 3 choices. The first digit \\(a\\) has 9 choices (1 through 9). Each of the other two digits \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) has 10 choices (0 through 9).\n\nSo, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 ending in 5 is:\n\\[\n9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 3 = 2700\n\\]\n\nAdding the two cases together, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\(B\\) is:\n\\[\nB = 2700 + 2700 = 5400\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(A + B\\)\nNow, we sum the number of four-digit odd numbers and the number of four-digit multiples of 5:\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 5400 = 9900\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9900}\n\\]",
