      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\( A \\), the number of four-digit odd numbers, we need to consider the range of four-digit numbers and the constraints on the last digit.\n\nA four-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of the following: 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. Therefore, the last digit can be chosen in 5 ways. The first digit (thousands place) can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 options), the second digit (hundreds place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 options), and the third digit (tens place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 options).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit odd numbers is:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\n\nNext, to find \\( B \\), the number of four-digit multiples of 5, we need to consider the range of four-digit numbers and the requirement that the last digit must be 0 or 5.\n\nFor a number to be a multiple of 5, its last digit must be either 0 or 5. Therefore, the last digit can be chosen in 2 ways. The first digit (thousands place) can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 options), the second digit (hundreds place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 options), and the third digit (tens place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 options).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 is:\n\\[\nB = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 2 = 1800\n\\]\n\nFinally, we need to find the value of \\( A + B \\):\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 1800 = 6300\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6300}\n\\]",
